{"text": " A payment system that works is table stakes, a payment system that is secure, compliant with Kenyan financial regulations, and passes Safaricom's production go-live review, is what separates a portfolio project from a production system that handles real money. In this lesson, security requirements for production Daraja integrations, credential management, IP white listing for Safaricom callback IPs, HTTPS enforcement, callback payload integrity, and the Safaricom go-live process, business verification, short code application, and production credential deployment. We cover credential hierarchy, consumer key slash secret, access token, security credential, initiate a name, what each is and how to protect each, IP white listing, restricting your callback endpoints to Safaricom's published callback IP ranges. The Safaricom production go-live checklist, what they audit before approving a production short code, and environment separation, never use production credentials in development, never use sandbox credentials in production. Production Daraja deployments have five security requirements that are non-negotiable for Safaricom's go-live approval. One, HTTPS everywhere. Your callback URLs must use HTTPS with a valid certificate. Self-signed certificates are not accepted. The most commonly failed Safaricom production audit requirement is the callback URL test. They literally post a test payload to your callback endpoints and check that you return the correct response format within three seconds. Developers who have only tested locally with NGROC often discover in the audit that their server is too slow or returning the wrong response format. Always test your callback endpoints with real network latency before applying for production. Remember this, production Daraja development rule. One environment equals one set of credentials. Never mix them. Never commit either to source control. Apply what you learn in this lesson and you will see results immediately. Your skills bear fruit.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 2.46, "text": " A payment system that works is table stakes,", "tokens": [50364, 316, 10224, 1185, 300, 1985, 307, 3199, 28429, 11, 50508], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.23986043321325423, "compression_ratio": 1.675, "no_speech_prob": 0.04320833832025528, "words": [{"word": " A", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.22, "probability": 0.5792386531829834}, {"word": " payment", "start": 0.22, "end": 0.5, "probability": 0.8749284744262695}, {"word": " system", "start": 0.5, "end": 0.9, "probability": 0.9969465136528015}, {"word": " that", "start": 0.9, "end": 1.16, "probability": 0.995535135269165}, {"word": " works", "start": 1.16, "end": 1.46, "probability": 0.9884394407272339}, {"word": " is", "start": 1.46, "end": 1.72, "probability": 0.9442943930625916}, {"word": " table", "start": 1.72, "end": 1.98, "probability": 0.9264734983444214}, {"word": " stakes,", "start": 1.98, "end": 2.46, "probability": 0.9131883978843689}]}, {"id": 1, "seek": 0, "start": 2.86, "end": 6.96, "text": " a payment system that is secure, compliant with Kenyan financial regulations,", "tokens": [50508, 257, 10224, 1185, 300, 307, 7144, 11, 36248, 365, 8273, 6277, 4669, 12563, 11, 50740], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.23986043321325423, "compression_ratio": 1.675, "no_speech_prob": 0.04320833832025528, "words": [{"word": " a", "start": 2.86, "end": 3.06, "probability": 0.9362742304801941}, {"word": " payment", "start": 3.06, "end": 3.32, "probability": 0.997238278388977}, {"word": " system", "start": 3.32, "end": 3.72, "probability": 0.9980282187461853}, {"word": " that", "start": 3.72, "end": 3.94, "probability": 0.998042106628418}, {"word": " is", "start": 3.94, "end": 4.06, "probability": 0.9963173866271973}, {"word": " secure,", "start": 4.06, "end": 4.52, "probability": 0.9928533434867859}, {"word": " compliant", "start": 4.94, "end": 5.32, "probability": 0.9958900809288025}, {"word": " with", "start": 5.32, "